xnu 0173: arm the virtual timer before unmasking it

fiq_context_init() writes CNTV_CTL_EL0 = ENABLE, clearing IMASK, without
programming CNTV_TVAL/CNTV_CVAL. The loader does not arm the timer either,
so the compare is whatever ABL left, usually in the past. The vtimer PPI is
level-sensitive: with an expired compare it stays asserted and re-enters the
handler for ever, and the boot never reaches the UFS probe. The diagnostic
shim hid this because its GIC test re-armed CNTV_TVAL before jumping; the
clean shim exposed it. Program a full tick before clearing IMASK; the timer
code reprograms the deadline from ml_set_decrementer() later.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c
@@ -2278,6 +2278,15 @@
 	 * IMASK so the level-sensitive PPI can reach EL1. Keep the GIC PPI
 	 * snapshots in 0110 to confirm delivery on the next boot.
 	 */
+	/*
+	 * The loader does not arm the virtual timer: CNTV_CVAL is whatever
+	 * ABL left, usually in the past. Clearing IMASK with an expired
+	 * compare makes the level-sensitive PPI assert for ever before the
+	 * first ml_set_decrementer(), which is the early boot storm. Give it
+	 * a full tick first, then unmask; the timer code reprograms it.
+	 */
+	uint64_t cntfrq = __builtin_arm_rsr64("CNTFRQ_EL0");
+	__builtin_arm_wsr64("CNTV_TVAL_EL0", cntfrq ? cntfrq / 100 : 240000);
 	uint64_t cntvctl = CNTV_CTL_EL0_ENABLE;
 #else
 	uint64_t cntvctl = CNTV_CTL_EL0_ENABLE; /* One wants to use 32 bits, but "mrs" prefers it this way */
